{"id":"x03","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"calendar","question":"Was January 19, 1639 a Monday (in the Gregorian calendar)?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"January 19, 1639 (proleptic Gregorian) was a Wednesday.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The Gregorian calendar repeats every 400 years, since 146,097 days is divisible by 7.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"January 1, 2000 was a Saturday, so January 1, 1600 was also a Saturday.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"From 1600 to 1639 there are 39 years including 10 leap years, giving 14,245 days.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"14,245 days is exactly 2,035 weeks, so January 1, 1639 was a Saturday.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"January 19 is 18 days after January 1, and 18 mod 7 = 4, so Saturday plus 4 days is Wednesday.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The Julian calendar, still used in many places in 1639, would give a different weekday but does not apply to this question.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.67},{"text":"The claim that January 19, 1639 was a Monday is false.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":5,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"}],"readout_weights":[0.25,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.25,0.25,0.0,0.25],"stages":[{"st